        "en" to GemDetails(
            "Rose quartz is a silicon dioxide, like rock crystal, coloured a soft pink. It mainly forms in the cores of granitic pegmatites, where it crystallises as large compact masses rather than crystals. Its colour, long attributed to titanium or manganese, actually comes from microscopic fibres of a dumortierite-like mineral distributed through the mass. The rare well-formed rose quartz crystals, found in Brazil, have a different origin: they grow at the end of pegmatite formation and owe their tint to defects linked to aluminium and phosphorus.",
            "Trigonal system. Mostly compact masses; prismatic crystals rare and small. No cleavage; conchoidal fracture. Hardness 7; specific gravity 2.65; vitreous to greasy lustre; translucent to cloudy, rarely transparent; refractive index 1.544 to 1.553; birefringence 0.009 (uniaxial positive). The fine rutile needles it sometimes contains give, in cabochon, a six-rayed star, best seen in transmitted light. Its colour may fade after long exposure to sun or heat. Durable, it is cut as cabochons, beads, spheres and carvings.",
            "Brazil: Minas Gerais and Bahia, the main source of massive rose quartz and the rare crystals. Madagascar (intense pink), Namibia, Mozambique, South Africa, India, Sri Lanka (star rose quartz), Japan and the United States (Black Hills, South Dakota, where it is the state mineral). Used in Mesopotamia and Egypt since Antiquity for seals and adornments.",
            "A stone of love in all its forms, gentleness and peace of heart, one of the best known in crystal healing, said to soothe, console and open one to tenderness. It is associated with the heart chakra and the signs of Taurus and Libra. Cleansing in water; recharging in moonlight rather than in the sun, which can fade it."
